# ============================================================
# REQUIRED — the app won't start without these.
# ============================================================

# Supabase (Project Settings → API)
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key

# Supabase service-role key. Bypasses RLS; used only by server-side
# routes (the webhook, automation engine, and the public API key
# auth path — see docs/public-api.md). Keep this secret — never
# paste it into client code.
#
# Note: the public API (/api/v1) needs no new env var. API keys are
# created in the dashboard (Settings → API keys) and stored hashed
# in the database; this service-role key is what lets the auth path
# look a presented key up without a user session.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your-service-role-key

# WhatsApp token encryption (64 hex chars = 32 bytes, AES-256-GCM).
# Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# Rotating this value orphans every token encrypted under the previous
# key — users have to re-save their WhatsApp settings to reconnect.
ENCRYPTION_KEY=your-64-char-hex-key-here

# Meta App Secret (Meta for Developers → App Settings → Basic).
# Verifies the HMAC-SHA256 signature on every inbound webhook POST.
# Required — without it the webhook rejects every request.
META_APP_SECRET=your-meta-app-secret

# ============================================================
# RECOMMENDED — safe defaults exist but you'll want to set these.
# ============================================================

# Canonical public URL of this deployment (scheme + host, no trailing
# slash). Used for the sitemap and OG images. Routes that need a
# self-referential URL (invite links from /api/account/invitations,
# any future email-bound link) derive the origin from the request
# itself, so this variable is *only* needed when the request-derived
# origin would be wrong — e.g. when generating links from a cron job
# or a background worker that has no incoming request.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://crm.example.com

# Default language locale (e.g. en)
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_LOCALE=en

# ============================================================
# OPTIONAL — only needed if you use the feature.
# ============================================================

# Defense-in-depth allow-list for the hostnames that
# /api/account/invitations is willing to publish in invite URLs.
# Comma-separated, no scheme, no port (just hostnames).
#
# Why this exists: when NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL is unset, invite URLs
# are derived from the incoming request's `Host` / `X-Forwarded-
# Host` header. On a typical proxied deploy the proxy sets these
# to your canonical hostname and they're trustworthy. On a *bare*
# deployment exposed to the public internet, an attacker could
# POST to the API directly with a spoofed `Host: phishing.example`
# and receive an invite URL pointing at their site.
#
# When this var is set, hostnames not on the list are rejected
# (the request falls through to the wacrm.tech fallback with a
# console.warn). When unset, behavior is unchanged from earlier
# versions — the request-derived host is trusted.
#
# Most operators don't need this: setting NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL to
# your canonical URL already pins invite links there. This is for
# operators who want belt-and-braces or run multi-tenant setups
# where one app instance serves several hostnames.
#
# Example:
#   ALLOWED_INVITE_HOSTS=crm.example.com,crm-staging.example.com

# Shared secret protecting GET /api/automations/cron, GET
# /api/flows/cron, GET /api/cron/hot-lead-alerts, GET
# /api/cron/lead-staleness-alerts, and GET /api/cron/ai-auto-resume.
# Required if you use Wait steps in automations (a scheduled pinger
# drains pending executions), want HOT-lead response-time alerts, want
# the pipeline's escalating "lead going cold" alerts, or turned on
# "Auto-resume after a handoff" in Settings → AI Assistant — every 5
# minutes is a good interval for any of these. Generate any long
# random string:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# See docs/docker.md.
# AUTOMATION_CRON_SECRET=generate-a-long-random-string

# Your auth.users.id (Supabase → Authentication → Users → copy the
# UUID) — grants YOU access to /agency, the agency-owner panel that
# shows every account/client on this instance at a glance (activity,
# leads, pipeline value, WhatsApp connection, inactivity). Nobody else
# can reach that route regardless of their account role — see
# src/lib/auth/agency.ts. Leave unset to disable the panel entirely
# (requests to /agency 404). Set this on BOTH the main deployment and
# the standalone one below, if you run one — it's the same check
# either place.
# SUPER_ADMIN_USER_ID=your-auth-users-uuid

# Set to "true" ONLY on a second, dedicated deployment of this same
# codebase that you want to serve nothing but the /agency panel on its
# own subdomain (e.g. agencia.tudominio.com) — a separate EasyPanel/
# Hostinger/etc. service pointed at the same Supabase project, with its
# own DNS record. When set, that instance redirects every route except
# /login and /agency away, so a stray visit there never renders a
# half-working copy of the client-facing CRM. Leave unset on your main
# deployment — this does not add security by itself (requireSuperAdmin
# already gates /agency everywhere), it only tidies up a second,
# dedicated instance's route surface. See src/middleware.ts.
# AGENCY_STANDALONE_MODE=true

# Meta App ID (Meta for Developers → App Settings → Basic). Required to
# create/edit message templates with an IMAGE header: Meta only accepts a
# Resumable-Upload media handle (not a plain URL) as the header sample, and
# that upload is app-scoped. Without it, image-header template submission
# returns a clear error; text/body-only templates are unaffected. Pair
# with META_APP_SECRET.
# META_APP_ID=your-meta-app-id

# When "true", POST /api/whatsapp/templates/submit skips the Meta call
# and stores the row with a synthetic `dry-run-<uuid>` meta_template_id.
# Set this in CI and local development so you can exercise the full
# template UI without a real WABA. Leave unset (or "false") in prod.
# WHATSAPP_TEMPLATES_DRY_RUN=true

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# AI reply assistant (optional)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The AI assistant is bring-your-own-key: each account pastes its own
# OpenAI or Anthropic key under Settings → AI Assistant. The key is
# stored AES-256-GCM-encrypted with ENCRYPTION_KEY (above) — there is
# NO global provider key env var, and nothing here is required for the
# feature to work. The two vars below only tune behaviour.
#
# The AI knowledge base (migration 030) uses Postgres full-text search
# out of the box. Optional semantic search needs the `pgvector`
# extension — migration 030 runs `CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS vector`
# (Supabase has it available) — plus a per-account embeddings key set in
# Settings → AI Assistant. Still no env var required.

# Per-call timeout for provider requests, in milliseconds. Default 30000.
# AI_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# How many recent text messages of a conversation to send the model as
# context (draft + auto-reply). Default 20.
# AI_CONTEXT_MESSAGE_LIMIT=20

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Coexistence webhook (e.g. Dualhook) — optional
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# A Coexistence provider registers with Meta as the connection's
# "business platform" and proxies inbound webhook deliveries to us —
# so Meta signs those payloads with the PROVIDER's app secret, not
# ours, and x-hub-signature-256 can't be verified against
# META_APP_SECRET. POST /api/whatsapp/webhook/dualhook/<secret> is a
# separate route for these connections, authenticated by this secret
# in the URL instead of an HMAC signature. Required only if you use a
# Coexistence provider; the direct-Meta webhook route is unaffected.
# Generate with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# DUALHOOK_WEBHOOK_SECRET=generate-a-long-random-string
#
# Note: which base URL outbound sends use for a given connection
# (e.g. https://api.dualhook.com/v25.0 instead of Meta directly) is
# NOT an env var — it's set per-connection in Settings → WhatsApp
# ("Send API Base URL"), since one instance can have some accounts on
# a Coexistence provider and others connected directly to Meta.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Zernio (WhatsApp / Instagram connect via Meta OAuth) — optional
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Powers "Connect" in Settings → Integrations
# (GET /api/zernio/connect/[platform] + GET /api/zernio/callback).
# Without ZERNIO_API_KEY, the Connect buttons fail with a clear
# "Zernio is not configured on this server" error rather than hanging.

# Server-side only — sent as `Authorization: Bearer <key>` to Zernio.
# Never exposed to the browser; the frontend always goes through our
# own /api/zernio/connect/[platform] route, never calls Zernio directly.
# ZERNIO_API_KEY=your-zernio-api-key

# Publicly reachable URL for GET /api/zernio/callback, e.g.
# https://crm.example.com/api/zernio/callback. Optional — when unset,
# it's derived from the incoming request's origin, which is fine for a
# single-domain deploy but won't work behind a proxy that changes the
# host Zernio sees. Set this explicitly once you have a stable public
# URL (same reasoning as NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL above).
# ZERNIO_REDIRECT_URL=https://crm.example.com/api/zernio/callback

# Override only for a Zernio sandbox/staging environment. Defaults to
# https://zernio.com.
# ZERNIO_API_BASE_URL=https://zernio.com

# Required for inbound WhatsApp messages to actually reach the inbox
# once someone connects through the button above — Zernio delivers
# them to POST /api/whatsapp/webhook/zernio, signed with this shared
# secret (its own X-Zernio-Signature HMAC scheme, unrelated to
# META_APP_SECRET / DUALHOOK_WEBHOOK_SECRET). Generate one value with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# then register the webhook subscription ONCE per deployment by running
# `node scripts/zernio-setup-webhook.js` (see that file's header
# comment for the exact env vars it needs). Skipping this step leaves
# the Connect flow itself working (accounts show as connected) but
# every inbound message silently drops — nothing calls this route.
# ZERNIO_WEBHOOK_SECRET=generate-a-long-random-string

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google Calendar (read/write the account's calendar) — optional
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Powers "Connect" in Settings → Integrations and Onboarding's
# calendar step (GET /api/integrations/google-calendar/connect + GET
# .../callback). Separate from "Continue with Google" login below —
# this is its own direct OAuth flow (not Supabase's), since it needs
# Calendar scopes and a refresh token this app stores itself.
#
# 1. In Google Cloud Console: create an OAuth 2.0 Client ID (Web
#    application), enable the Google Calendar API, and add
#    https://<your-domain>/api/integrations/google-calendar/callback
#    as an authorized redirect URI.
# 2. Set the two values below from that client.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your-client-secret

# Publicly reachable URL for the callback above. Optional — same
# reasoning as ZERNIO_REDIRECT_URL: unset derives it from the incoming
# request's origin, which won't work behind a proxy that changes the
# host Google sees.
# GOOGLE_CALENDAR_REDIRECT_URL=https://crm.example.com/api/integrations/google-calendar/callback

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sentry (error monitoring) — optional
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# When unset, Sentry is never initialized (client, server, or edge) —
# no error reporting, no runtime cost, no code path change. See
# src/instrumentation-client.ts and src/instrumentation.ts.
#
# 1. Create a free project at https://sentry.io (platform: Next.js).
# 2. Copy its DSN (Settings → Projects → <project> → Client Keys) into
#    the value below. A DSN is not a secret — it's meant to ship in
#    client-side bundles — but it's still an env var so each deployment
#    (self-hosted operators included) can point at its own project.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=https://xxxx@xxxx.ingest.sentry.io/xxxx

# The three below are ONLY used at build time, to upload readable
# (un-minified) source maps to Sentry so stack traces in the dashboard
# show real file/line numbers instead of minified output. Skipped
# silently when unset — the build and error reporting both work fine
# without them, you'll just read minified traces in Sentry.
# SENTRY_ORG=your-org-slug
# SENTRY_PROJECT=your-project-slug
# Sentry → Settings → Auth Tokens → create one with `project:releases`
# scope. Keep this secret — never expose it to the browser.
# SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=your-auth-token

# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# "Continue with Google" login — optional
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not an env var here: enable the Google provider in your Supabase
# project's Dashboard → Authentication → Providers, using a Client
# ID/Secret from the same (or a separate) Google Cloud OAuth client as
# above, with https://<project-ref>.supabase.co/auth/v1/callback added
# as an authorized redirect URI on that client. Supabase handles the
# rest — /login and /signup's "Continue with Google" button just calls
# supabase.auth.signInWithOAuth(), no server-side code here needed.
